Movie Recommendation System: Exploratory Data Analysis & Feature Engineering

Project Overview
This project focused on the critical data preparation and Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) phase foR building a robust Movie Recommendation System. Using the MovieLens 100K dataset, I moved beyond a simple rating matrix to engineer sophisticated features that capture user behavior, item popularity, and temporal trends.

Objectives
Perform deep data cleaning and merging of multiple relational datasets (Movies, Ratings, Tags).
Engineer new features to quantify and mitigate common recommendation system biases (e.g., user leniency and popularity bias).
Analyze temporal drift to understand how user tastes evolve over time.
Define a roadmap foR a prediction model including regularization strategies.

Technical Workflow
Data Wrangling: Merged ratings.csv and movies.csv on movieId.
                Converted Unix timestamps into readable Datetime objects foR time-series analysis.
                Handled missing values and ensured data consistency across 100,000+ records.

Feature Engineering:
User Metrics: Calculated user_avg_rating to identify "strict" vs. "lenient" raters.
Item Metrics: Created popularity (rating count) and average_rating per movie.
Temporal Features: Extracted release_year and rating_year to analyze the impact of movie age on ratings.
Genre Analysis: Deconstructed pipe-separated genres to count multi-genre tags per movie.

Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA):
Visualized rating distributions using Matplotlib and Seaborn.
Analyzed the "Long Tail" effect iN movie popularity.
Compared average ratings of "New Movies" (2000+) vs. "Old Classics" (Pre-2000).

Key Insights
User Bias: A small group of highly active users tends to rate more critically than the average user, necessitating normalization.

Content Popularity: 10% of movies account for nearly 50% of the total ratings, confirming the "popularity bias" that recommendation algorithms must balance.

Temporal Drift: Average ratings show a slight decline over time, suggesting that older movies iN the dataset are often rated more favorably (survivorship bias).

Tools & Libraries
Python: Core programming language.
Pandas & NumPy: For data manipulation and matrix operations.
Matplotlib & Seaborn: For statistical data visualization.
